            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

As discussed, your house has some significant air leakage associated with the older metal windows and the chimney (for the furnace &amp; hot water tank), You are planning to eliminate the current mechanical equipment (and chimney), replace all of the windows, and extensively renovate with spray foam insulation in the basement, main floor &amp; perhaps the ceiling as well. This will significantly tighten up the house to the point where having appropriate mechanical ventilation will be important. If it was being built today, there would be a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit installed &amp; I talk further about this is the Ventilation section of this report.



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 115 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.7 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. Based on your renovation plans, I think it is highly likely the reduction in air leakage will be closer to 30% or higher, tripling the energy savings &amp; increasing the rebate to $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 (R12) and have 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene insulation on the exterior. We discussed your plans to strip the interior walls, remove the existing insulation &amp; wiring ........ and start from scratch.



You are planning to replace the interior R12 batt with 3" (R16.4) of spray foam insulation and the exterior rigid insulation will be increased from 1.5" (R7.5) up to 3" (R15).

      - Net increase of R11.9



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding another R11.9, as outlined above.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R11.9, you could potentially save $ 160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), The estimated savings may appear a bit low, however you need to fold in the energy savings of the window upgrades (part of the walls) as outlined separately within this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.



Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The concrete basement walls are insulated on the inside (with 2x3 - R8) and the upper 40% is above grade and has the 1.5" (R7.5) rigid foam insulation &amp; siding (same as the upper main floor). The header space above the concrete has R12 insulation inside &amp; the R7.5 rigid insulation outside.



We talked about your plans to:



1. Strip the interior basement walls down to the bare concrete &amp; add 2.5" (R13.5) of spray foam insulation against the concrete and build an interior 2x4 wall for the electrical. This 2x4 wall will also have R12 batt insulation. The upper 40% of the wall will have the exterior rigid insulation increased from 1.5" (R7.5) up to 3" (R15).

     - Net increase of R17.5 to lower 60% and R25 to upper 40%

2. The header space will have 5" (R27.6) of spray foam insulation and the exterior rigid insulation from 1.5" (R7.5) up to 3" (R15).

     - Net increase of R23.1



Savings: By completing the above insulation upgrades, you could potentially save $ 390 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,250 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

You house has 14 windows and most of them are older original windows. In conjunction with your planned wall insulation upgrade, this would be a good time to upgrade all of the windows with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified window rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 469 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.2 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate per window (12 windows maximum rebate = $3,000) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is expected to become quite air tight in conjunction with your planned renovations. Almost all houses from the 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. As your basement will be completely "gutted" the opportunity for you to install an HRV is still quite good !



As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 70% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



Ultimately ............ you need to rely on your local heating &amp; ventilation contractor to design &amp; install a ventilation system that keeps your home healthy &amp; comfortable !!</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an older 150 litre propane-fired hot water tank, co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. 



The new system you are contemplating is a 95% AFUE "combi" gas boiler, that will provide both the heating &amp; domestic hot water for your home. Please see my upgrade recommendation &amp; energy/cost savings within the previous section about the Heating System.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient gas furnace. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, that will provide both the heat and domestic hot water for your home.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler, you could potentially save $ 1131 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 48.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,250   from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one in the basement is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $15,250 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $2,250 for the basement insulation

     - $   500 for the ceiling insulation

     - $3,000 for the windows

     - $2,500 for the heating system

     - $1,000 for a 30% air leakage reduction ($500 or $750 if you achieve a 10% or 20% reduction)

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate and the basement + ceiling insulation, however these rebates would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR washing machine - $200

     - ESTAR refrigerator - $200

     - ESTAR dishwasher - 50% up to $200

     - ECM circulation pumps for your new "combi" boiler - 50% up to $175 each (up to 5 per home)

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



I was not able to complete an exhaust devices depressurization test (which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment), as the kitchen range hood fan does not vent outside, the clothes dryer was not connected and neither bathroom fan was working,



Your renovation plans will eliminate the current furnace &amp; hot water tank (and the chimney they co-vent up through) and will also eliminate the basement woodstove (that vents out its' own chimney). There is currently a 6" combustion air supply pipe in the basement that serves the furnace, hot water tank &amp; wood stove. This combustion air supply pipe may become a fresh air supply for your new ventilation system (your heating &amp; ventilation contractor to design this).



During my assessment of your home, I did not see any smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - The clothes dryer was not connected

        - Neither bathroom exhaust fan operates.



If your new ventilation design still includes bathroom exhaust fans (vs. having the bathroom exhaust integrated through an HRV system) than please choose ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fans that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $0.62 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



